<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Oboi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1610">1610</a>–<a href="/years/1669">1669</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Aobai</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Oboi was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchu</a> military commander and courtier of the Gūwalgiya clan who served three emperors of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>. Registered under the Bordered Yellow Banner, he won a hereditary captaincy and the honorific Baturu, brave warrior, in the wars against the Ming, and fought in the Qing conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> from 1644. In 1661 the dying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunzhi-emperor">Shunzhi Emperor</a> named him one of four regents for the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a>; Oboi reversed Shunzhi's policies and pushed to reassert Manchu power over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a>. In 1669 the Kangxi Emperor had him arrested on 30 charges and imprisoned, and he died that year; he was later rehabilitated, with a pardon in 1713.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oboi.jpg" alt="Oboi" width="330" height="513" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous Qing court painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Oboi.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>The Gūwalgiya clan had served the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchus">Manchus</a> since Oboi's grandfather Solgo submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a> in 1588. His father Uici was once garrison commander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shenyang">Mukden</a>, and his uncle Fiongdon was one of Nurhaci's most trusted generals. As a third son Oboi was not to inherit the family's hereditary post. In 1637, during the second Manchu campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, he volunteered to take Ka Island south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yalu-river">Yalu River</a>, a Ming base, and after a hard landing and hand-to-hand fighting the garrison was annihilated. In 1641 he won five encounters in the sieges of Jinzhou and Songshan.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-taiji">Hong Taiji</a>'s death in 1643 Oboi backed his eldest son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hooge">Hooge</a> for the throne against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorgon">Dorgon</a>. He fought on through the conquest, was made a general in 1645 and campaigned against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-zicheng">Li Zicheng</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-xianzhong">Zhang Xianzhong</a>; the Draft History of Qing says he killed Zhang in battle in 1647, but other Qing records tell the story without him. When Dorgon, as regent, purged the Yellow Banners in 1648, Oboi was sentenced to death for conspiring to make Hooge emperor; the sentence was commuted to loss of rank. After Dorgon's death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunzhi-emperor">Shunzhi Emperor</a> restored him, made him a cabinet minister and, in 1652, commander of the imperial bodyguard, in which post he arrested and executed nobles of Dorgon's faction.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shunzhi-emperor">Shunzhi Emperor</a> died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> on 5 February 1661 and named four regents for his eight-year-old son: Sonin, Suksaha, Ebilun and Oboi. While the ageing Sonin held the balance, the regency worked; as his health failed, Oboi dominated Ebilun and pushed Suksaha aside. Before he died Sonin petitioned the young emperor to take personal rule early, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kangxi-emperor">Kangxi Emperor</a> did so on 25 August 1667, but Oboi still forced him to have Suksaha and his family executed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Arrested and sentenced to death, Oboi had his sentence reduced to imprisonment in view of his service; some sources say he showed the emperor the wounds he had received defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nurhaci">Nurhaci</a>. He died on 27 June 1669. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongzheng-emperor">Yongzheng Emperor</a> made him a First Class Duke with the posthumous title Chaowu, exceedingly martial, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> lowered him to First Class Baron. He appears as a scheming regent in Jin Yong's novel The Deer and the Cauldron.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Oboi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
